SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 317 | Monday, March 23, 2026

Good morning.

1) Core capital goods orders up 0.8% in February, the fifth straight rise
Machinery and electrical equipment led. Combined with the ISM new-orders reading and the LTL tonnage numbers, this is the most consistent set of industrial-demand signals in three years. The transportation-equipment component was the exception, down 4%, which is the truck and trailer weakness showing up in the national data.

2) The largest US 3PL guides to flat revenue and higher margin
The investor day message was that the company will let low-margin brokerage volume go to competitors and keep the contract-logistics and managed-transportation business. The CEO used the word "software" 41 times, by our count, and "trucks" nine times.

3) Oakland's Saturday gate: 540 moves, no line
The terminal called the first Saturday "a success"; truckers called it "fine." The real test is the second half of April when the almond and stone-fruit export volume peaks. Several Central Valley shippers told us they will move bookings back from LA/LB if the gate holds.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 1,040. Warehouse-management-system implementations that went live in North America last year, per a market tracker published this morning, a 15-year low. Everyone bought during 2021-2022 and is now trying to make it work.

That's the morning. Reply with tips.

